---
/**
 * foodcraft / LocationBO — "VISIT US" (Sipsip Tea Bar / BO).
 *
 * ROLE: the getting-here section. The design draws a CENTRED head — a lilac
 * eyebrow pill over the address set as the Baloo 2 headline — and under it two
 * SEPARATE rounded cards sitting in a 1.25fr / .75fr grid with a real 16px
 * gutter between them: a drawn map face on the left, an opening-hours card on
 * the right. Nothing is sharp: both cards take the template's large corner and
 * every small label on them is a 100px pill.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html", data-component="LOCATION" (id="visit"):
 *   head    text-align center · margin-bottom 30px
 *           eyebrow  lilac ground · deep-purple label · 11px 700 · .14em caps
 *                    padding 8px 16px · radius 100px
 *           h2       clamp(28px,3.6vw,46px)/1.1 · margin .24em 0 0 · ink
 *   grid    minmax(0,1.25fr) minmax(0,.75fr) · gap 16px
 *   map     min-height 380px · radius 28px · overflow hidden · ground #F4EAF8
 *           plot grid 44px at rgba(123,79,168,.09)
 *           roads  top 47% h:6px · left 44% w:5px · rgba(123,79,168,.2)
 *           pin    translate(-50%,-100%) off that crossing
 *           dot    16px #7B4FA8 ringed 4px #FFF7F2
 *           flag   9px under the dot · #241A22 / #FFF7F2 · 11px 700
 *                  padding 7px 14px · radius 100px
 *           caption left 18px bottom 18px · #FFF7F2 ground · #5D5158 label
 *                  11px 700 · padding 7px 14px · radius 100px
 *   card    #FFFFFF · radius 28px · padding 28px 30px
 *   row     flex · space-between · gap 14px · padding 11px 0 · 1px #F4EEF1
 *           day 13.5px 600 #6B6067 · time 14px 700 #241A22, right
 *   note    13px/1.7 #6B6067 · margin 15px 0 0
 *
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE (R1). Every value above is written as the
 * PageBO token that already holds it: --paper --card --well --lilac --line
 * --ink --on-ink --ink-soft --muted --accent --accent-deep --accent-rgb --sans
 * --r-lg --r-pill. THREE of the design's hexes have NO exact token, and each is
 * reported rather than invented:
 *   · the map ground #F4EAF8. Nearest is --well / --lilac (#EEE2F5) — every
 *     channel within 8/255 — and it is the token PageBO already paints behind
 *     an image (.plate) and behind the eyebrow pill, so the cell stays in the
 *     same lilac family the design puts it in. AboutBO made this same call for
 *     the same design's #F0E4F6 photo ground.
 *   · the hours hairline #F4EEF1. Nearest is --line (#EEE2F5), within 12/255,
 *     and --line is also the semantic token for exactly this: a rule between
 *     rows. It arrives through the wrapper's .rowrule primitive.
 *   · the two 28px card corners. --r-lg is 26px — PageBO deliberately
 *     normalised this design's 22–28px radii into one token, and writing 28px
 *     here would make this the only section in the template off it.
 *
 * NO BAND. The design draws LOCATION on the plain blush ground, so this section
 * carries neither .band-pink, .band-sage nor .band-lilac; the pink and sage
 * grounds are spent elsewhere on this page. The purple never touches the pink
 * here because the pink is not on this section at all.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * so the sidebar owns a real input for each — a data-field on an undeclared path
 * is a dead hook that focuses nothing):
 *   location.tag                      — the lilac eyebrow pill (the wrapper's .kick)
 *   location.headline                 — the h2, AND THE ADDRESS. Rendered with
 *                                       set:html so an owner's own <em> picks up
 *                                       the design's PURPLE emphasis.
 *   location.rules.<original i>.label — the DAY cell of an hours row
 *   location.rules.<original i>.value — the TIME cell of an hours row
 *   location.hours                    — the card's closing note
 *   footer.locality                   — the map caption (the design's "shopTown").
 *                                       The schema's own comment at that field
 *                                       names its three consumers as the header
 *                                       tag, the footer wordmark AND THE MAP
 *                                       CAPTION, so this is its declared use.
 *                                       HeaderBO binds the same path: one
 *                                       sidebar input, two click targets on the
 *                                       page — not two inputs fighting.
 *   location.lat / location.lng       — feed the Leaflet boot. No data-field:
 *                                       they are coordinates, not copy, and the
 *                                       sidebar edits them as their own inputs.
 *
 * WHY location.rules AND NOT location.hours FOR THE DAY/TIME ROWS. The schema
 * declares `location.hours` as a single kind:'text' field ("Hours line") and
 * `location.rules` as the kind:'list' of {label,value} pairs. The design's card
 * is a REPEATING two-cell row, so it needs the list. Emitting
 * `location.hours.<i>.day` instead is not a style choice, it is the exact data
 * loss the schema's own comment warns about and that isSchemaEditablePath was
 * written to stop: LocationBI shipped that path, the deep writer converted the
 * existing `location.hours` STRING into an array, and every other hours row
 * vanished on the next rebuild.
 *
 * THE CARD HAS NO HEADING, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. The
 * design opens the card with a literal "Open hours" h3 and closes it with a
 * literal caveat paragraph; both are the DESIGNER filling in their own template
 * (R2), so neither ships as written and only one of the two slots has a hook
 * behind it. location.hours takes the CLOSING NOTE, not the heading, for one
 * concrete reason: an owner who types only an hours line and adds no rows then
 * gets a card that still states their hours. Put the same string in the heading
 * and that owner gets a 21px Baloo 2 title with nothing underneath it — a
 * heading over nothing, which is the thing R2 exists to prevent. No other schema
 * path owns a title for this card, and location.tag is already spent on the
 * section eyebrow above.
 *
 * DERIVED, printed but NOT editable here:
 *   footer.brand || layout.businessName → the pin flag (the design's "shopName").
 *   It carries no data-field on purpose, and for a reason specific to this
 *   section rather than a general one: the flag lives INSIDE #map, and PageBO's
 *   boot runs `el.innerHTML = ''` there the moment Leaflet mounts. A hook inside
 *   a subtree the page erases at runtime is a hook that disappears from under
 *   the editor. HeaderBO owns the editable copy of the same value.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of these reads as an oversight:
 *   · location.sub. Declared in the schema and used by siblings, but this
 *     design's LOCATION head is eyebrow + h2 and nothing else — there is no
 *     third element in it. A paragraph under a CENTRED headline is a different
 *     head than the one that was handed over.
 *   · location.address as a second printed line. The design's h2 IS the address
 *     (`{{ addressLine }}`); there is no separate address block anywhere in this
 *     section. The value is still read below — it is tier 2 of the map, which is
 *     how this section finds its own pin — and FooterBO prints it, so
 *     location.address keeps a live sidebar input either way.
 *   · location.phone. The design's LOCATION prints no number; the closing band
 *     and the footer own it. A tel: row here would be a control this design does
 *     not have (R2).
 *   · location.directions.text/href. Schema-declared and used by siblings, but
 *     this design's LOCATION draws no button. Adding one would be building a
 *     different section than the one that was handed over.
 *   · NO .plate / .ph / role="img" (R9). The map cell holds no upload — it is a
 *     DRAWING of a map — so it gets neither the wrapper's image frame nor an
 *     image role. The only accessible name available for it would be a sentence
 *     I wrote ("Map showing …"), which is exactly the component-supplied copy R2
 *     and R9 forbid. The whole drawn face — grid, roads, pin and flag — is
 *     therefore aria-hidden ONCE, on the face itself and nowhere else; a literal
 *     aria-hidden beside a conditional one is the duplicated-attribute bug two
 *     earlier components shipped. Everything the drawing pictures is announced
 *     as real text beside it: the address headline, the caption and the rows.
 *   · NO STAR ROW (R2). This section carries no rating field and no review. Five
 *     stars printed for a customer who gave no rating is a fabricated claim;
 *     stars belong to TESTIMONIALS and only ever from a numeric rating field.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER (R7), and this too is a decision. The
 *     helper exists to rewrite in-page hrefs onto this page's ids, and this
 *     section emits NO href at all — the map is not a link, the cards are not
 *     links, and the design draws no button here — so a copied helper would ship
 *     as dead code that later drifts out of step with the wrapper. AboutBO and
 *     TrustBO made the same call for the same reason. The anchor duty this file
 *     DOES own is honoured: id="visit", exactly as the design writes it and
 *     exactly the anchor PageBO's nav targets. If a link is ever added here,
 *     copy the helper from HeaderBO with PAGE_ANCHORS = menu · about · gallery ·
 *     visit · faq · main and ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location,contact → visit ·
 *     services,rooms → menu · host,why → about · work → gallery · book → main,
 *     passing tel: / mailto: / http through untouched.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). Body content ONLY: the map (which needs real coordinates OR
 * a real address behind it), the hours rows, or the hours note. NOT the tag and
 * NOT the headline — lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets location.tag = 'Visit'
 * and location.headline = 'Where to find us' on EVERY site, so a gate that
 * counted them would be always-true and would ship an eyebrow and a heading over
 * an empty gutter on every first build. The head is gated again on its own pair,
 * and each of the two cards is gated separately, with `is-one` collapsing the
 * grid to a single track so the survivor takes the full width instead of sitting
 * in a 1.25fr column beside nothing.
 *
 * THE MAP, three tiers, the pattern the family shares:
 *   1. build-time lat/lng → window.__foodcraftBOInitMap(lat, lng, label)
 *   2. else any address   → client-side Nominatim lookup, then the same boot
 *   3. else               → the drawn plot-grid face, which IS the design
 * The drawn face lives INSIDE #map until Leaflet mounts; PageBO's boot runs
 * el.innerHTML = '' first, and that is what erases it. The literal id="map" and
 * the exact global name __foodcraftBOInitMap are a contract with PageBO — rename
 * either and the drawn face silently stays on screen underneath the tiles.
 *
 * A CLEARED coordinate field leaves '', which passes != null and which Number('')
 * turns into 0 — booting Leaflet and pinning the shop at 0,0 in the Gulf of
 * Guinea with its own name on the marker: a map asserting a location the owner
 * never gave. num() rejects blank and out-of-range values so a cleared field
 * falls through to the address lookup instead, and it uses ?? rather than || so
 * a genuine 0 survives.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE IS THE ONE PLACE THIS FILE RESTATES A FONT-SIZE, and it is the
 * design's own: LOCATION is set at clamp(28px,3.6vw,46px) where PageBO's shared
 * .h2 is clamp(28px,3.9vw,52px). The design steps this headline down from the
 * clamp(30px,4vw,52px) it uses on six other sections, and that step is the
 * difference between an address line and a sales headline. AboutBO takes the
 * shared primitive because ITS design value is 52px and the primitive already
 * prints it; here the primitive would print 6px too large at a wide viewport.
 *   R8's clamp trap was checked at every boundary rather than assumed. The
 * restated clamp is declared ONCE, at base, and is touched by no media query in
 * this file: it evaluates 46px at 1278px and above, 43.2px at 1200, 32.4px at
 * 900, and its 28px floor at every width at or below ~778px — monotonic, never
 * growing as the viewport narrows. A scoped class out-specifies the wrapper's
 * :where(.h2) — Astro's default 'attribute' scoping makes `.sip-visit-h2`
 * (0,2,0) against that rule's (0,1,1) — so this rule knowingly takes the shared
 * 760 pass's font-size off this one heading. Nothing is lost by that: the
 * shared pass is clamp(26px,7vw,29px), which resolves to its 29px cap at 760px
 * where the base .h2 hands over 29.68px at 761px, so the shared ladder steps
 * DOWN across that boundary too. Both are monotonic; this one is simply the
 * design's own. The same pass's `max-width: none` is NOT restated here and
 * still lands.
 *
 * FOUR OTHER VALUES RESTATED OFF A WRAPPER PRIMITIVE. None is a new choice; each
 * puts back a number the design states and the primitive rounds off or is
 * unaware of.
 *   · The eyebrow is a PILL here — lilac ground, deep-purple label, 8px/16px,
 *     100px corner — and its tracking is .14em where .kick's base is .18em. Its
 *     FONT-SIZE is deliberately NOT restated, so the wrapper keeps the whole
 *     size curve including its 520 step to 10.5px.
 *   · The h2 is CENTRED, so its margin is `.24em auto 0` rather than the
 *     design's `.24em 0 0`. The wrapper's 22ch measure is a block width, and a
 *     block narrower than its parent does not centre itself; without the auto
 *     margins a short address would sit left of a centred eyebrow. The cap
 *     itself is the wrapper's and is not re-invented.
 *   · The row cells, the flag and the caption take `line-height: normal`. The
 *     design sets no leading on any of them, so `normal` is literally what it
 *     draws, where PageBO's body leading of 1.72 leaks in and inflates an hours
 *     row from the drawn ~41px to ~46px. `normal` rather than a number, because
 *     a number would be a guess where this is the value.
 *   · The caption is NOT the .mono primitive. .mono is tracked uppercase at
 *     .16em; the design's caption is plain sentence-case 11px/700. A town name
 *     shouted in tracked caps is a different mark.
 *
 * ONE FORCED DEVIATION, and it is the same one every map cell in this family
 * makes: the pin flag DROPS `white-space: nowrap`. The map cell clips overflow
 * to keep its 26px corner, so a long business name would be sliced off at both
 * ends; wrapping beats truncating on the element that names the shop. The stack
 * is centred on the 44% crossing, so it is capped at twice the smaller side —
 * 88% — less 12px of air each side, and `width: max-content` is required
 * alongside that or absolute shrink-to-fit collapses it to (containing block
 * − left). The caption needs no such deviation: the design already gives it an
 * opaque --paper backing, which is what keeps it readable once CARTO tiles are
 * painted under it.
 *
 * BOTH ROW CELLS ARE GUARDED, which is where this file departs from its BM
 * sibling. BM renders an empty span for a missing cell because the row is
 * space-between and dropping the day cell would promote the time cell to the
 * left edge. Removing the ELEMENT is what R2 asks for, so the time cell carries
 * `margin-left: auto` instead: with both cells it is redundant against
 * space-between, and with only a time it still lands on the right edge. An empty
 * span with a data-field on it is also a zero-width click target, so nothing is
 * lost for the editor. The guard that matters still runs in the frontmatter,
 * where a wholly blank row is dropped along with its hairline.
 *
 * VERIFIED CONTRAST — COMPUTED for this section's own pairs (sRGB relative
 * luminance, WCAG 2.x), not estimated:
 *   eyebrow  --accent-deep on --lilac   7.40
 *   headline --ink on --paper          15.92
 *   day cell --muted on --card          6.01   note --muted on --card 6.01
 *   time     --ink on --card           16.86
 *   caption  --ink-soft on --paper      7.13   (on its opaque pill)
 *   flag     --on-ink on --ink         15.92
 * Nothing in this section is below AA — on the NATIVE palette, which is what
 * these six figures are and not a guarantee. buildOverrideCss writes
 * --accent-deep from palette.primaryDeep and remaps --muted to palette.inkSoft,
 * so an admin scheme pick moves both. In five of the six pairs BOTH sides are
 * engine-owned and move together; the eyebrow is the one mixed pair, because
 * --lilac is one of the tokens the engine never writes. The pair PageBO warns
 * about — --accent on --pink at 3.93 — cannot occur here: there is no pink in
 * this section.
 *
 * NO INTERACTIVE ELEMENT. This section renders no link, button, summary or
 * tabindex, so the wrapper's focus ring is never exercised here and R8's
 * full-width-button clause has nothing to apply to. Every text cell that can
 * receive a long unbroken owner string takes overflow-wrap: anywhere instead.
 *
 * SECTION PADDING is the wrapper's, not this file's. The design writes
 * `max-width:1200px;margin:0 auto;padding:76px 28px 0`: the 1200/gutter half is
 * the shared .wrap, and the top is `html[data-page="BO"] section`'s
 * clamp(48px,6.4vw,80px), which caps 4px above the design's 76px.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const lay = layout || {};

const loc = content.location || {};
const tag = typeof loc.tag === 'string' ? loc.tag.trim() : '';
// Nothing beyond trim(): the headline goes out through set:html so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches PageBO's purple <em> rule.
const headline = typeof loc.headline === 'string' ? loc.headline.trim() : '';
// The card's closing note. The schema's single "Hours line" text field — see the
// frontmatter for why it lands here and not in the card's heading.
const hoursNote = typeof loc.hours === 'string' ? loc.hours.trim() : '';

// READ, NOT PRINTED. The design's head is eyebrow + h2 and its h2 IS the
// address; there is no second address block in this section. This value feeds
// the geocode below (tier 2 of the map) and FooterBO prints it.
const address = String(loc.address ?? lay.contact?.address ?? '').trim();

// A row the owner added but never filled would spend a hairline and 22px of
// padding saying nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts as blank) but carry
// the ORIGINAL index so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3 (R6),
// and note that this filter is what makes the gate honest: a rules array of
// nothing but blanks no longer keeps the card alive.
type HourRow = { i: number; day: string; time: string };
const rows: HourRow[] = (Array.isArray(loc.rules) ? loc.rules : [])
  .map((r: any, i: number) => ({
    i,
    day: String(r?.label ?? '').trim(),
    time: String(r?.value ?? '').trim(),
  }))
  .filter((r: HourRow) => Boolean(r.day || r.time));

const num = (v: any, max: number): number | null => {
  if (v === null || v === undefined || String(v).trim() === '') return null;
  const n = Number(v);
  return Number.isFinite(n) && Math.abs(n) <= max ? n : null;
};
const lat = num(loc.lat, 90) ?? num(lay.contact?.lat, 90);
const lng = num(loc.lng, 180) ?? num(lay.contact?.lng, 180);
const hasCoords = lat !== null && lng !== null;
const hasAddress = !!address;
// The drawn face is a picture claiming where this shop physically is. With
// neither coordinates nor an address there is nothing real behind that claim, so
// the whole cell leaves and the hours card takes the full width.
const hasMap = hasCoords || hasAddress;

// The pin flag and the marker popup. No placeholder: with no business name the
// flag leaves and the pin is a bare dot, rather than a label reading "Your
// Business" over a real street. `||`, never `??`, so a CLEARED footer.brand
// falls through to the business name instead of printing ''.
const brand = String(content.footer?.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// The design's "shopTown". Editable, and the schema field's own comment names
// the map caption as one of its three consumers.
const locality = String(content.footer?.locality ?? '').trim();

const hasCard = rows.length > 0 || !!hoursNote;
const hasHead = !!tag || !!headline;
const hasVisit = hasMap || hasCard;
---
{hasVisit && (
<section class="sip-visit" id="visit">
  <div class="wrap">
    {hasHead && (
      /* TWO children, exactly as the design draws it: the pill and the address.
         No sub-paragraph — see the frontmatter. */
      <div class="sip-visit-head reveal">
        {tag && <span class="kick sip-visit-kick" data-field="location.tag">{tag}</span>}
        {headline && <h2 class="h2 sip-visit-h2" data-field="location.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
      </div>
    )}
    {/* TWO separate rounded cards with a real gutter — not one bordered frame.
        `is-one` collapses the grid to a single track when only one of them
        survives its guard, so the survivor takes the full width. */}
    <div class:list={['sip-visit-grid', 'reveal', (!hasMap || !hasCard) && 'is-one']} data-delay="1">
      {hasMap && (
        <div class="sip-visit-map">
          <div id="map">
            {/* Tier-3 only, and entirely decorative: PageBO's boot empties #map
                before Leaflet draws, so tiles never stack on top of this. One
                aria-hidden, here and nowhere else in the subtree. */}
            <div class="sip-visit-face" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="sip-visit-road sip-visit-road-h"></span>
              <span class="sip-visit-road sip-visit-road-v"></span>
              <span class="sip-visit-pin">
                <span class="sip-visit-dot"></span>
                {brand && <span class="sip-visit-flag">{brand}</span>}
              </span>
            </div>
          </div>
          {/* OUTSIDE #map on purpose: the town is the owner's real text, not
              fallback chrome, so it has to survive the boot that erases the
              face — and it stays in the accessibility tree. */}
          {locality && <span class="sip-visit-cap" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
        </div>
      )}
      {hasCard && (
        <div class="sip-visit-card">
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <div class="rowrule sip-visit-row">
              {r.day && <span class="sip-visit-d" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.label`}>{r.day}</span>}
              {r.time && <span class="sip-visit-t" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.value`}>{r.time}</span>}
            </div>
          ))}
          {hoursNote && <p class="sip-visit-note" data-field="location.hours">{hoursNote}</p>}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
{hasCoords && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ lat, lng, brand }}>
    (function () {
      // Bounded like the geocode branch below: this script is emitted where the
      // section sits, ABOVE the wrapper's boot, so one retry is the normal case —
      // but an unbounded loop would spin a timer forever on any page where the
      // wrapper script never ran at all.
      var attempt = 0;
      (function init() {
        if (window.__foodcraftBOInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBOInitMap(lat, lng, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(init, 80); }
      })();
    })();
  </script>
)}
{!hasCoords && hasAddress && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ address, brand }}>
    (function geocodeThenInit() {
      var attempt = 0;
      function tryInit(la, ln) {
        if (window.__foodcraftBOInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBOInitMap(la, ln, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(function () { tryInit(la, ln); }, 80); }
      }
      try {
        var query = encodeURIComponent(String(address).replace(/\n/g, ', '));
        var url = 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&limit=1&q=' + query;
        fetch(url, { headers: { 'Accept-Language': 'en' } })
          .then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : []; })
          .then(function (results) {
            if (!Array.isArray(results) || results.length === 0) return;
            var la = parseFloat(results[0].lat);
            var ln = parseFloat(results[0].lon);
            if (isNaN(la) || isNaN(ln)) return;
            tryInit(la, ln);
          })
          .catch(function () { /* keep the drawn face: it is the design */ });
      } catch (e) { /* never block render */ }
    })();
  </script>
)}
<style>
  /* ── THE HEAD · centred, 30px to the grid. Straight from the design, which
        puts the eyebrow and the address on one axis and nothing beside them. */
  .sip-visit-head { text-align: center; margin: 0 0 30px; }

  /* THE EYEBROW. The wrapper's .kick already carries the design's type — 11px,
     700, uppercase, inline-block — and it owns the whole size curve (it steps to
     10.5px at 520), so no font-size is written here. What .kick does NOT know is
     that in THIS design the eyebrow is a PILL: a lilac ground, the deep purple
     label, 8px/16px of padding and the 100px corner. Those four and the design's
     .14em tracking (the wrapper's base is .18em) are the only things restated.
     max-width + overflow-wrap keep one long owner word — a hyphenless place
     name — from pushing the document sideways at 360px (R8). Qualified with
     .sip-visit-head so it out-specifies the wrapper's :where() primitive
     whatever order the sheets land in. */
  .sip-visit-head .sip-visit-kick {
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    background-color: var(--lilac);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The design's OWN display step for this section: clamp(28px,3.6vw,46px),
     smaller than the shared .h2's clamp(28px,3.9vw,52px). Declared once, at
     base, and touched by no media query in this file — see the frontmatter for
     the boundary-by-boundary check and for why this is the monotonic ladder
     rather than the shared one. line-height 1.12 and --ink come from the
     primitive; so does the 22ch measure, which is kept and only CENTRED here:
     a 22ch block inside a centred parent does not centre itself, so `auto`
     replaces the design's zero side margins. */
  .sip-visit-h2 {
    margin: .24em auto 0;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 46px);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* set:html output carries no scope attribute, so an owner's <em> inside the
     headline is only reachable through :global() (R3). PageBO already paints
     .h2 em and .h2 .em purple globally; this pair restates the wrapper's own
     values so the emphasis survives even if that global rule is ever narrowed,
     and invents nothing. font-style is NORMAL on purpose: Baloo 2 has no
     italic, and asking for one synthesises an oblique that fights its rounded
     terminals — the emphasis in this design is a COLOUR change. */
  .sip-visit-h2 :global(em),
